In a pi extension, “search” works by registering a **custom tool** with `pi.registerTool()`. The LLM can then call that tool like built-in `web_search`.

Basic flow:

1. Extension loads from:
   - `~/.pi/agent/extensions/search.ts`, or
   - project `.pi/extensions/search.ts`
2. Extension calls `pi.registerTool({ name: "my_search", ... })`
3. The tool schema defines inputs, e.g. `{ query, count }`
4. In `execute()`, your code calls a real search API
5. Return text/results to the model

Example Brave Search extension:

```ts
import type { ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { Type } from "typebox";

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  pi.registerTool({
    name: "brave_search",
    label: "Brave Search",
    description: "Search the web using Brave Search API",
    promptSnippet: "Search the web for current information using Brave Search.",
    parameters: Type.Object({
      query: Type.String({ description: "Search query" }),
      count: Type.Optional(Type.Number({ description: "Number of results" })),
    }),

    async execute(_toolCallId, params, signal) {
      const apiKey = process.env.BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY;
      if (!apiKey) throw new Error("Missing BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY");

      const url = new URL("https://api.search.brave.com/res/v1/web/search");
      url.searchParams.set("q", params.query);
      url.searchParams.set("count", String(params.count ?? 5));

      const res = await fetch(url, {
        signal,
        headers: {
          "Accept": "application/json",
          "X-Subscription-Token": apiKey,
        },
      });

      if (!res.ok) {
        throw new Error(`Search failed: ${res.status} ${await res.text()}`);
      }

      const data = await res.json();

      const results =
        data.web?.results?.map((r: any, i: number) =>
          `${i + 1}. ${r.title}\n${r.url}\n${r.description ?? ""}`
        ).join("\n\n") ?? "No results";

      return {
        content: [{ type: "text", text: results }],
        details: data,
      };
    },
  });
}
```

Then set the API key:

```bash
export BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY="your_key"
```

Reload pi:

```text
/reload
```

After that, the model can call `brave_search`.

Your earlier `web_search` failed because the configured Google Custom Search API project returned `403`. An extension can avoid that by using another provider like Brave, Tavily, Kagi, SerpAPI, etc.

